descriptions.value:
Affected versions of MISP’s interactive CLI shell implement access control independently from the normal web application, causing several authorization inconsistencies.


The patch shows that CLI access could differ from the web application in multiple security-sensitive areas:

 - feed listings did not enforce the same lookup_visible restrictions for non-host-organisation users;
 - feed detail access did not enforce the same host-organisation/site-admin authorization as FeedsController::view();
 - Feed.headers, which can contain HTTP authorization credentials, could be exposed instead of being hidden or masked;
 - server synchronization authkey values were not explicitly hidden from CLI detail output;
 - sharing-group detail access did not consistently use SharingGroup::checkIfAuthorised();
 - the use command could establish context for a record without first proving that the user was authorized to view that record


The commit additionally hardens pagination and terminal rendering, including neutralization of terminal control sequences found in database-backed values. Those are important hardening changes, but the main vulnerability is the CLI authorization/data-disclosure mismatch.

Version affected: ≤2.5.45

### Summary



`qs.stringify` with `allowEmptyArrays: true` writes the key of an empty array without encoding it, even though keys are encoded by default. A key that contains `&` (or the configured `delimiter`), `=`, or `#` therefore becomes query string syntax instead of data, so a caller-supplied key name can add parameters to the serialized query string, or cut it short.



### Details



In `lib/stringify.js`, the `allowEmptyArrays` branch returns `adjustedPrefix + '[]'` early, before the code that passes every other key through `encoder` and `formatter`. So `{ "x&k=v": ["1"] }` serializes as `x%26k%3Dv%5B0%5D=1`, but `{ "x&k=v": [] }` serializes as `x&k=v[]`. The early return applies at any depth (`{ "a": { "b&k=v": [] } }` gives `a[b&k=v][]`), under every `arrayFormat`, with `allowDots`, and with a custom `encoder`, which is never called for that key. With `encode: false` or `encodeValuesOnly: true`, keys are intentionally left unencoded on every path; those configurations are not affected.



#### PoC



```js



var qs = require("qs");



var query = qs.stringify({ q: "hi", "x&apiKey=evil&y": [] }, { allowEmptyArrays: true });



// "q=hi&x&apiKey=evil&y[]"



qs.parse(query);



// { q: "hi", x: "", apiKey: "evil", y: [""] }



qs.stringify({ "x#": [], z: "1" }, { allowEmptyArrays: true });



// "x#[]&z=1": in a URL, everything after "#" becomes the fragment



```



#### Fix



Applied in 234d505 on `main` and released as v6.17.0, and backported to v6.16.2, v6.15.4, v6.14.3, v6.13.4, and v6.12.6: the key of an empty array goes through `encoder` and `formatter` exactly as the key of any other value does, so `{ "x&apiKey=evil&y": [] }` now serializes as `x%26apiKey%3Devil%26y[]`. Keys that need no encoding serialize as before, with two exceptions that match every other path: the brackets of a nested key are now encoded (`a[b][]` becomes `a%5Bb%5D[]`, which `qs.parse` reads identically), and with `encodeDotInKeys`, a dot in the key becomes `%252E` instead of `%2E` (which `decodeDotInKeys` previously misread as nesting).



### Affected versions



- `>=6.12.0 <6.12.6`, fixed in v6.12.6



- `>=6.13.0 <6.13.4`, fixed in v6.13.4



- `>=6.14.0 <6.14.3`, fixed in v6.14.3



- `>=6.15.0 <6.15.4`, fixed in v6.15.4



- `>=6.16.0 <6.16.2`, fixed in v6.16.2



v6.17.0 and later are not affected.



`allowEmptyArrays` was introduced in f22b2bc and first shipped in v6.12.0, with this early return. Earlier versions have no `allowEmptyArrays` option and are not affected.



### Impact



An application that passes an object whose key names come from untrusted input to `qs.stringify` with `allowEmptyArrays: true` (for example, to forward a parsed JSON body or user-selected fields to an upstream service) can have parameters injected into the resulting query string. The injected parameters are not keys of the object, so deleting or overwriting a key on the object (such as setting a trusted `apiKey` after copying the user's fields) does not remove them; whether the attacker's value or the application's wins depends on how the receiving side handles a duplicate parameter. A `#` in such a key ends the query string early when the result is placed in a URL. `allowEmptyArrays` is off by default, and only keys whose value (after any `filter`) is an empty array are affected.

solutions.value:
Upgrade to tauri-plugin-http 2.7.0 or later and turn on the new scopeRedirects option. Both steps are needed. The fix is opt-in, so 2.7.0 on its own still follows a redirect out of scope; the option is what makes the plugin check every hop. Tauri made it opt-in because a redirect that leaves the scope now fails, which changes behaviour for apps that were relying on it.

    {"plugins": {"http": {"scopeRedirects": true}}}


Nothing in the 2.0.0 to 2.6.1 range has a fix available.

workarounds.value:
Apply the patch, and configure the default Cache-Control as needed.

For deployments that cannot apply the patch, a method modifier of _serve_static in the Catalyst class can be used to override the Cache-Control or Expires headers as needed, for example:

    around _serve_static => sub {
        my ( $next, $c, $path, $cache_control ) = @_;

        $c->$next($path);

        my $config = $c->config->{'Plugin::Static::Simple'};
        $cache_control ||= $config->{cache_control} || "public";

        if ( defined $config->{expires} ) {

            if ( $config->{expires} <= 0 ) {
                $cache_control = 'no-store';
                $c->res->headers->expires(0);
            }
            else {
                $c->res->headers->expires( time() + $config->{expires} );
            }
        }

        $c->res->headers->header( 'Cache-Control' => $cache_control );
        return 1;
    };

Users who cannot immediately upgrade can mitigate this issue by updating their templates to use the str:eval extension method instead of the {#eval} section helper.
Vulnerable pattern: {#eval snippet /}
Secure alternative: {str:eval(snippet)}

Applications using efi_loadopt_is_valid() should validate the size of the input buffer before passing it to libefiboot. As a library-level fix, the device path iterator should enforce a minimum node Length of 4 before recursing:

  if (dp->length < 4)
      return -1;

